{
  "id": "area-of-circle-oq-01-oq-02-oq-02-oq-02-oq-01",
  "name": "The k-th derivative Fourier identity ĉₙ(f⁽ᵏ⁾) = (i·n)ᵏ·ĉₙ(f)",
  "parentId": "area-of-circle-oq-01-oq-02-oq-02-oq-02",
  "status": "completed",
  "knowledge": {
    "score": 9,
    "insights": [
      "Isoperimetric (Hurwitz Fourier) lineage. Grandparent oq-02-oq-02 proves the first-order IBP identity ĉₙ(f') = i·n·ĉₙ(f); parent oq-02-oq-02-oq-02 iterates once for ĉₙ(f'') = −n²·ĉₙ(f). This child CLOSES THE INDUCTION: ĉₙ(f⁽ᵏ⁾) = (i·n)ᵏ·ĉₙ(f) for smooth periodic f and every k.",
      "This is the spectral statement behind the whole Fourier approach: differentiation acts diagonally on the Fourier basis with eigenvalue i·n, so d^{2m} has eigenvalue (i·n)^{2m} = (−1)^m·n^{2m}.",
      "Proof = clean induction on k. Step rewrites f⁽ᵏ⁺¹⁾ = (f')⁽ᵏ⁾ via Function.iterate_succ_apply, applies the IH to the smooth periodic function f' (universally quantifying f INSIDE the ∀ so the IH is available at deriv f), then peels one factor i·n with the grandparent first-order identity. Smoothness preserved by contDiff_infty_iff_deriv; periodicity preserved by parent's deriv_periodic_of_periodic.",
      "Smoothness uses ContDiff ℝ ∞ (C^∞), dodging the finite-C^k succ/cast bookkeeping. KEY API in Lean v4.26 Mathlib: ContDiff smoothness lives in WithTop ℕ∞ where ∞ = ((⊤:ℕ∞):WithTop ℕ∞) is NOT the order-top ⊤ (=ω, analytic). So `1 ≤ ∞` is NOT le_top; use `hf.of_le (by exact_mod_cast le_top)` (mathlib's own idiom). ∞ notation is `open scoped ContDiff`. ContDiff.iterate_deriv / contDiff_infty_iff_deriv / contDiff_infty (↔ ∀n:ℕ, ContDiff n) are the smooth-deriv toolkit.",
      "Corollaries: fourierCoeffOn_deriv2_smooth recovers the parent's −n² as k=2 ((I·n)²=−n² via Complex.I_sq); fourierCoeffOn_iteratedDeriv_even gives the d^{2m} eigenvalue (−1)^m·n^{2m} (mul_pow+pow_mul+I_sq).",
      "Bilinear (polarized) Parseval on an interval is Mathlib-absent: Mathlib provides only the diagonal hasSum_sq_fourierCoeffOn (‖ĉₙ‖²). The cross-term ∑ₙ Re(ĉₙ(f)·conj ĉₙ(g)) = (2π)⁻¹∫f·g follows by polarization from the diagonal via the parallelogram identity ‖u+v‖²−‖u−v‖²=4Re(u·conj v) applied to the coefficients of f±g.",
      "fourierCoeffOn has no additivity lemma in Mathlib (only fourierCoeffOn.const_smul). Additivity/subtractivity for continuous ℂ-valued functions follows from the integral form fourierCoeffOn_eq_integral + interval-integrability of fourier(-n)•F; coe continuity = (map_continuous (fourier (-n))).comp (AddCircle.continuous_mk' _).",
      "SECTION XII (equality case / spectral rigidity): saturating the analytic Wirtinger inequality 2∫f·g′=∫(f′²+g′²) forces EVERY Fourier mode |n|≥2 to vanish (ĉₙf=ĉₙg=0), so f,g are degree-≤1 trig polynomials = the circle. Forward direction of Hurwitz equality-iff-circle.",
      "Mechanism: the Hurwitz deficit is a HasSum of nonnegative per-mode terms with total (2π)⁻¹[∫(f′²+g′²)−2∫f·g′]; under saturation total=0, so le_hasSum forces every summand=0; sharpened bound (|n|²−|n|)(‖a‖²+‖b‖²)≤summand (gap=|n|(‖a‖−‖b‖)²) then kills |n|≥2 modes since |n|²−|n|≥2>0.",
      "SECTION XIII: the Section-XII equality case only forces modes |n|≥2 to vanish, leaving a first-harmonic curve = ELLIPSE in general; the genuine CIRCLE requires the n=1 deficit summand to also vanish, giving ĉ₁f = i·ĉ₁g (a 90° rotation) — the Cauchy–Schwarz/AM–GM equality signature of the circle at the fundamental frequency.",
      "First-mode rigidity is a clean sum-of-squares identity: ‖a‖²+‖b‖²−2·Im(a·conj b) = (a.im−b.re)²+(a.re+b.im)², so vanishing ⟺ a.im=b.re ∧ a.re=−b.im ⟺ a = i·b.",
      "SECTION XIV converse: from the circle spectral signature (all modes |n|>=2 dead and c1f=I*c1g) every Hurwitz deficit summand vanishes, so total deficit=0 => Wirtinger saturation 2*int f*g' = int (f'^2+g'^2); full analytic + geometric equality-IFF-circle now COMPLETE, all 0-axiom.",
      "Reality relation conj(cn(f))=c(-n)(f) for real f (fourierCoeffOn_conj_neg) is the key new lemma: conjugation passes through the real (b-a)^-1 scalar (Complex.conj_ofReal) and the interval integral (integral_conj on Ioc via integral_of_le), fixes the real integrand, and flips the kernel conj(fourier(-n))=fourier n (fourier_neg); handles the n=-1 mode not covered by hone/hhigh.",
      "n=-1 deficit summand: with c(-1)=conj c1, (conj a * conj conj b).im = -(a * conj b).im (since conj a * b = conj(a*conj b)); the (-1)^2 and -2*(-1) signs combine to reproduce the n=1 summand => 0 via firstMode_deficit_zero.",
      "Quantitative isoperimetric stability (Bonnesen/Fuglede-type): the Hurwitz deficit is not merely nonnegative-with-equality-at-the-circle but CONTROLS each higher harmonic. Extracting one nonnegative term from the deficit HasSum (le_hasSum) and bounding it below via the sharpened per-mode gap (area_deficit_summand_ge_gap, coefficient |n|²−|n|≥2 on |n|≥2) yields 2·(‖ĉₙf‖²+‖ĉₙg‖²) ≤ normalized deficit — a mode-by-mode L²-stability estimate that has the qualitative equality-iff-circle rigidity as its degenerate (deficit=0) case.",
      "SECTION XVI aggregate stability: summing the sharp per-mode Fuglede gaps gives a SINGLE global inequality (2π)²·∑_n(|n|²−|n|)(‖ĉₙf‖²+‖ĉₙg‖²) ≤ L²−4πA. The weight |n|²−|n| vanishes on the three circle modes n∈{−1,0,1}, so the LHS is the total L²/H¹-distance of the curve from the family of circles — the deficit dominates ALL harmonics summed, not one at a time.",
      "Aggregation is a comparison-test argument on the deficit HasSum: gap summand is 0≤gap_n (deficit_gap_coef_nonneg: |n|²−|n|=|n|(|n|−1)≥0 for integer n) and gap_n≤deficit_n (gap_le_deficit_summand = area_deficit_summand_ge_gap in the exact shape of (HSf.add HSg).sub (HSA.mul_left 2)); Summable.of_nonneg_of_le gives summability, Summable.tsum_le_tsum dominates the tsum by the deficit total.",
      "SECTION XVII: the standard textbook Fuglede stability inequality strips the H¹ frequency weight |n|²−|n| to a plain L²-distance-to-circle bound. Key: |n|²−|n|≥2 uniformly on |n|≥2, so 2·(unweighted tail energy) ≤ weighted gap sum ≤ deficit. Constant 2 is sharp (attained as |n|→2).",
      "SECTION XVIII closes the honesty gap of SECTIONS XV-XVII: those docstrings only *asserted* the Fourier tail was an L²-distance-to-a-circle. tsum_tail_eq_sub_low is the abstract engine — for any HasSum a T over ℤ, the |n|≥2 tail sums to T - (a(-1)+a 0+a 1); the low band {-1,0,1} is finitely supported (hasSum_sum_of_ne_finset_zero) and subtracts off the full HasSum.",
      "Fed Mathlib Parseval (hasSum_sq_fourierCoeffOn_real) into that engine: the plain unweighted tail ∑_{|n|≥2}‖ĉₙg‖² EQUALS (2π)⁻¹∫g² − (‖ĉ₋₁‖²+‖ĉ₀‖²+‖ĉ₁‖²) — a literal integral, i.e. the squared L² distance of g from its best degree-≤1 trig fit (the circle coordinate). No overclaim: RHS is total energy minus low-mode energy = Bessel distance.",
      "The plain-L² Fuglede constant 2 (SECTION XVII) is EXACTLY the minimum of the harmonic weight w(n)=|n|²−|n| over higher modes |n|≥2: attained at |n|=2 (w=2), strict (w>2) for |n|≥3. This upgrades the asserted \"sharp constant 2\" docstring claim to a proved optimality theorem — raising the constant breaks the termwise bound for any curve with a nontrivial second harmonic."
    ],
